00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:10.000
There is something about grief. It distorts time and space.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.000
I can’t tell you what happened after that day.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Memories come back to me in waves, broken, scattered, unreal.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.000
I must have found a way to cross the channel.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.000
I must have found a way to make it to Britain.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.000
I probably followed other birds, though I don’t remember any of them.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:42.000
All I remember is, in my heart, I was alone.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.000
And then, one day, I found myself in London.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:52.000
After weeks of mourning, the city hit me in the face like a bad dream.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.000
I landed on a wide, crowded square.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:01.000
There were humans everywhere, walking in all directions, too busy to look at us.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:07.000
There were animals too, four giant lions, but they were made of stone.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:13.000
Everything was grey, the ground, the buildings, the people, the sky.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:25.000
Even the other birds: there were hundreds of them, fat, plump creatures perched on every monument. Clearly, they were the masters of this place.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:32.000
I looked around but couldn’t see any trees. Just towers of stone and glass, as high as mountains.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:42.000
I couldn’t breathe. The air just felt different. It had a cloudy, metallic smell. It was cold. Oh, so cold.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:52.000
I couldn’t understand. Was this really the Eldorado the travellers had spoken about? Had it all been a joke?

00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:01.000
I felt dizzy. That can’t be it, I thought. I haven’t travelled across two continents for this.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.000
There must be something good about this place.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.000
There must be.
